{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":175340,"duration_api_ms":170980,"num_turns":26,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"19387bee-66de-478d-a886-b5931abc363e","total_cost_usd":1.0884305000000003,"usage":{"input_tokens":20,"cache_creation_input_tokens":62788,"cache_read_input_tokens":1079061,"output_tokens":6255,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":62788,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":20,"outputTokens":6255,"cacheReadInputTokens":1079061,"cacheCreationInputTokens":62788,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.0884305000000003,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"amendments":[{"body":"Several NO3 envelope bounds in medium_planted and warm_room scenarios are extremely narrow — e.g., (4.0, 5.5), (4.5, 5.5), (4.5, 6.0) — representing only 1–1.5 mg N/L of range. These track the source water nitrate concentration (moderate.toml: 5.0 mg N/L) rather than meaningful biological behavior. Downstream beads (tanksim-6e5.2.3, 2.4, 2.5) will normalize nitrogen-cycle kinetics and plant uptake, which will shift NO3 dynamics. Widen these bounds to at least ±2–3 mg/L (e.g., (2.0, 8.0)) so they catch real regressions without breaking on every parameter tune. Document that NO3 bounds for moderate/warm_room scenarios include a source-water contribution of ~5 mg N/L.","rationale":"The acceptance criteria explicitly states envelopes should be 'broad enough to survive tuning but narrow enough to catch regressions.' A 1 mg/L NO3 window will fail the moment anyone touches nitrogen-cycle parameters, source water profiles, or plant uptake rates — all of which are planned for Phases 1–3. These tests would become maintenance burden rather than regression guardrails."},{"body":"Envelope::check silently passes NaN values in all f64 fields (pH, DO, TAN, NO2, NO3, reproductive_readiness, plant_biomass, algae_nuisance, biofilter_maturity, periphyton_biomass, fast_stem_biomass) because NaN comparisons return false in IEEE 754. Add a NaN/Inf guard at the top of the check method: for every constrained f64 field, if the snapshot value is not finite, push a violation like 'pH is NaN (expected [{min}, {max}])'. The existing final_finite assertions only catch NaN at the end of multi-hour runs, not at intermediate checkpoints where the root cause would be easier to diagnose.","rationale":"This is a correctness gap. If a system produces NaN partway through a run (e.g., division by zero in a new nitrogen kinetics formula), the envelope check would silently pass all intermediate checkpoints, and the NaN would only be caught at the final_finite assertion at the very end — far from the originating tick, making root cause diagnosis much harder. The bead's own comment convention says the harness 'on failure prints metric=X, expected=[min, max], at tick=N' — but NaN defeats this diagnostic goal."},{"body":"The maintained_medium_variant_reaches_reproduction_window test zeroes out both shrimp_base_mortality_per_day and shrimp_stress_mortality_scale, forces periphyton biomass to ≥5g, forces adult reserve to ≥3g, forces reproductive_readiness to ≥0.5, and extends maturation to 120 days. This amount of state manipulation means the test doesn't protect the natural reproduction pathway — it only proves reproduction can happen under artificially perfect conditions. Add a doc comment explicitly stating this is a 'controlled ideal' test (not a naturalistic baseline), and consider adding a companion test with at most mortality zeroed (no other manipulations) to verify that at least adult survival is achievable under maintained-tank conditions without rigging reserves and readiness.","rationale":"Downstream beads (tanksim-6e5.6.6: shrimp scenario tests for breeding/heat stress/crash) depend on this bead's reproduction envelope as a baseline. If the baseline only passes under heavily manipulated conditions, downstream tests can't trust it as evidence that the normal reproduction code path works. The acceptance criteria says 'reproduction behavior explicitly preserved where still desired' — the current test preserves that it's possible, but not that it's reachable from natural state evolution."}],"summary":"All 12 harness tests compile and pass. The new Envelope fields (juveniles_count, berried_females_count, shrimp_reproductive_readiness), the from_state constructor, the DO peak/trough rewrite, and the reproduction scenario are structurally sound. Three issues found: (1) nitrate envelope bounds for moderate/warm_room scenarios are dangerously narrow (1–1.5 mg/L range tracking source water concentration), likely to break when downstream beads tune nitrogen-cycle parameters; (2) Envelope::check silently passes NaN values due to IEEE 754 comparison semantics, defeating intermediate diagnostic value; (3) the reproduction test relies on heavy state manipulation (zero mortality, forced reserves/readiness) rather than testing the natural reproduction pathway, limiting its value as a regression baseline for downstream shrimp-focused beads."},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"a63a60f5-8003-4376-aa3c-433b396b2051"}
